ually?","options":["A single transpose already leaves every matrix unchanged","Transposing twice gives the inverse of the original","Flipping rows and columns twice restores the original","Flipping twice rotates the matrix by a half turn"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9a6ccf857de7c9e4aa3ce304","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Can a non-square matrix be equal to its transpose?","options":["Yes, every matrix equals its transpose.","No, because $A$ and $A^{\\top}$ have different sizes unless the matrix is square.","No, because transposes are undefined for non-square matrices.","Yes, whenever the matrix has a zero entry."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"82ffa2d2d0633bed375bfc9e","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A$ be $2\\times3$ and $B$ be $3\\times4$. What is the size of $(AB)^{\\top}$?","options":["$4\\times3$","$2\\times4$","$3\\times3$","$4\\times2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cccd6c6390536140f9c1f90c","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $(A^{\\top})^{\\top}$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}4&3\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&3\\\\2&4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"badd5044daa70200c44a3e22","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $A$ is $2\\times3$ and $B$ is $3\\times2$, what is the correct expression for $(AB)^{\\top}$?","options":["$B^TA^{\\top}$","$A^TB$","$BA$","$A^TB^{\\top}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a50c6ac25c41198cd8f3ed72","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does transposition turn row vectors into column vectors?","options":["It multiplies the vector by the identity matrix.","It deletes all but the first entry.","It changes every entry into its reciprocal.","A $1\\times n$ matrix becomes an $n\\times1$ matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1ac2d3b487fed7a41d70c5c4","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $A^{\\top}=A$, what special kind of square matrix is being suggested?","options":["A permutation matrix necessarily.","A symmetric matrix.","A non-square matrix.","A zero matrix necessarily."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c5834647c9b00bb628c374d5","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A$ be $3\\times2$ and $B$ be $2\\times5$. Which product equals $(AB)^{\\top}$?","options":["$B^TA^{\\top}$, of size $5\\times3$","$A^TB$, of size $2\\times5$","$A^TB^{\\top}$, of size $2\\times2$","$B^TA$, of size $5\\times2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c55e46eae61c25db7fdd5787","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&0\\\\3&4&5\\end{pmatrix}$, which entry of $A$ becomes the $(1,2)$ entry of $A^{\\top}$?","options":["$a_{11}=1$","$a_{22}=4$","$a_{21}=3$","$a_{12}=2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f8832008cba11fe90c1f86ce","time_limit":40}]}}